From: BookReel <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] fix(browsers): do not default `detached` to true on Windows

`launch()` spawns the browser with both `detached: opts.detached` and
`windowsHide: true`. libuv maps those onto DETACHED_PROCESS and
CREATE_NO_WINDOW respectively, and Win32 documents CREATE_NO_WINDOW as
ignored when it is combined with DETACHED_PROCESS.

So on Windows the default `opts.detached ??= true` silently cancels the
`windowsHide: true` set four lines below it. The browser starts with no
console to inherit, allocates its own, and that console is visible: every
headless launch drops a black window onto the user's desktop. A tool that
fans out to several browsers drops several, and they steal focus as they
appear.

Measured on Windows 11 x64, Node 22.22.0, @puppeteer/browsers 3.2.1,
puppeteer-core 25.8.0, chrome-headless-shell 152.0.7977.30:

  a single puppeteer-core launch()   stock: +3 visible windows   patched: +0
  a real multi-browser render        stock: 16 windows           patched:  0

Only the default changes, so a caller that explicitly asks for
`detached: true` on Windows still gets it.

---
 packages/browsers/src/launch.ts | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/packages/browsers/src/launch.ts b/packages/browsers/src/launch.ts
--- a/packages/browsers/src/launch.ts
+++ b/packages/browsers/src/launch.ts
@@ -154,7 +154,14 @@
         // `detached: true` makes child process a leader of a new process group,
         // making it possible to isolate process trees and prevent console signal propagation.
         // @see https://nodejs.org/api/child_process.html#child_process_options_detached
-        opts.detached ??= true;
+        //
+        // NOT on Windows, where it is actively harmful: libuv maps `detached`
+        // to DETACHED_PROCESS and `windowsHide` to CREATE_NO_WINDOW, and Win32
+        // ignores CREATE_NO_WINDOW when DETACHED_PROCESS is also set. Defaulting
+        // this to true there silently cancels the `windowsHide: true` set below,
+        // and every browser lands a visible console window on the user's
+        // desktop. An explicit `detached: true` from a caller is still honoured.
+        opts.detached ??= process.platform !== 'win32';
         const stdio = this.#configureStdio({
           pipe: opts.pipe,
         });
